ese non-standard variables  as field names are inconsistent and data  must be reviewed manually  often alongside the provider themselves  before a comprehensive understanding of  the data is reached  so after parsing existing data sets and  consulting with davi partners  a database compatible json structure was  created to harmonize common metadata  fields  we've worked towards programming custom  parser pipelines for each project  allowing for ingestion of the  unstructured data into the harmonize  format  from there users can easily build  cohorts from harmonized variables within  dabi  shown in green in the figure our  ultimate goal with these harmonization  efforts is to reduce dataset  heterogeneity  while still minimizing investigator  burden following harmonization  multi-site studies will be made  available for query and cohort creation  allowing for increased statistical power  and evaluations of device safety and  implant efficacy  including identifying candidate patients  target brain regions  and recording and stimulation parameters  across sites  thanks so much for listening hi  my name is martin luszczynski i'm a  postdoc working at columbia university  i use electrophysiology across different  scales of neural organization  to understand mechanisms of cognition  when we study cognition we often do so  by asking participants to hold their  eyes still  while the experimenter is presenting  bits of information right in front of  their eyes  while this approach has been very  fruitful it misses important aspects of  natural vision  in natural vision we actively explore  our surroundings  it is our intention intentions goals and  interests that largely determine  what will fixate next and importantly at  which points in time will fixate it  in my work i combine intracranial  recordings in human epilepsy patients  with simultaneous eye tracking during  natural reviewing  this allows me to explore the influence  of eye movements on a distributed  network  and study both local neural processing  patterns and global network interactions  together with my colleagues at nathan  klein institute in new york we also  conduct similar experiments in non-human  primates  which allows us developing explanations  that bridge between species  a typical experiment might require  participants to look at a set of images  you see one example here or we might  give them a simple task  like to memorize the details of an image  or simply to ask whether they like an  image or not  by measuring simultaneously eye tracking  and neural activity  we can detect points of fixations and  eye movements and use them to sort the  neural data  you can see an example fill potentials  with fixation points  superimposed in the upper right panel  and fixation related phase coherence  in the lower left panel both images show  that fixations occur at a very specific  time points in relation to the field  potential  in the current work i am presenting at  this meeting i used such an approach to  evaluate the possibility that  circades influence large-scale neural  dynamics and operate beyond the visual  system  to our surprise we found that eye  movements impact indeed extends way  beyond the visual system to sub-critical  structures like non-visual thalamus  or even classical auditory critical  areas  you can see more details on my posterior  tta13  altogether this outlines a very exciting  theoretical proposition  that the eye movements structure not  only visual but also other sensory  perception like the auditory perception  i hope that by studying human brain in  the context of natural continuous and  active sampling behavior  we could develop a more comprehensive  understanding of both  brain and cognition  hi i'm christine a graduate student from  the clark lab at northeastern university  today i'll be talking about dna based  nanosensors for magnetic resonance  imaging  of acetylcholine in the brain the  project goal is to develop  nanostructured sensors  in this case mri contrast agents for  detecting  and in vivo monitoring of  neurotransmitters in the brain  imaging tools that enable real-time  visualization of molecular neural events  are valuable for understanding the basis  of brain function and disease  current methods such as  electrophysiology that utilizes  microelectrodes and optical imaging  have the ability to record neural  activity in the brain with relatively  high specificity and accuracy  however there can be added benefits to  this brain imaging toolbox  such as better spatial resolution and  monitoring chemical signals at a higher  scale  in this work we have developed an  acetylcholine responsive  mri nano sensor as shown in the sensor  design  we have a dna scaffold approximately 20  nanometers  with bi-sites for attaching the ph  sensitive gadolinium contrast agent  and acetylcholine catalyzing enzymes  the acetylcholine catalyzing enzymes  here butyl chlorine esterase  causes a local decrease in the ph by  hydrolyzing the acetylcholine into  acetic acid  which is then detected by the ph  sensitive contrast agent in close  proximity  leading to a measurable change in the  relaxation rate  ultimately giving off a stronger signal  intensity in the presence of  acetylcholine  dna based ph sensitive and acetylcholine  responsive  magnetic resonance nanosensors were  successfully prepared and shown to be  sensitive at various ranges of  acetylcholine concentration  this sensor provides a non-invasive  method for monitoring acetylcholine  and helps advance our understanding of  molecular neural signaling in the brain  thank you  hi so i think we're back live now um so  we have some questions a few questions  coming in  and i would like to ask the audience if  you have questions please feel free to  enter them in the question and answer  box and we will get to them in turn  the first question is for aaron and the  question has to do  the question has to do with the  accuracy and the range of sensitivity of  your ph based sensor  aaron are you on yes thank you for your  question  from the blinded test i showed in the  figure on my slide  we were able to calculate ph uh within  .05  ph units of the actual ph  which was measured by a second  researcher using a ph electrode  and i do believe that it's quite  accurate  if you consider the  range  we're looking at ranges that are  measured from  the physiological ph and sort of going  down closer to three or four because  that is where  the range for protonation deprotonation  of our sensor occurs  great okay thank you and and a follow  one question for that  is your calibration measurements uh  these were done  um in vitro or actual actual uh  biological tissue  so far the calibration measurements that  we use to create  the image analysis analysis equations  and that standard curve  are done in vitro using a relaxometer  so we can get very careful ph  measurements  of those samples and have the best  standard curve possible  terrific thank you very much  let's see the next question go ahead  michelle please  oh go ahead um our next question was a  follow-up to the bis  web and is there a tutorial that people  can use  thanks for the question yes we have  under each panel we  have a help menu and if you click on  that we provided a brief manual on how  to use  the corresponding uh panel  including connectivity viewer and we  have also youtube channel  that you can follow all those videos  and how to use biscuit  thank you thank you so much for that  question  okay this next question is for nicole um  are you seeing a similar trend in delta  band power for the other participants  with ocd  hi yes thank you for the question um we  have looked at one other participant so  far who's been in the study for a  shorter period  of time and so far we've observed an  increase in delta van power  over six weeks in the left vcbs for this  participant  but not the right ucvs and in the future  we plan to do electrolocalization to  figure out if electro placement might  explain this difference  thank you nicole morrison the next  question is for you  do you know the laminar origin of the  beta signal that you discussed  uh yeah so when human we don't you know  we usually record the  uh with the laminar resolution but um  based on previous  uh um monkey physiology i would expect  that to be primarily  in the superficial layers  all right thank you thank you this  question is for christine  the brain also changes ph locally how do  you distinguish ph sensitive  versus acetylcholine sensitivity with  your  sensor  hi um that is something  that i haven't considered thus far in my  research and um  i will make sure to look into that more  okay thank you for the answer though  um livia what is the chemical basis of  your oxygen  sensor and as a follow-up how is that  then incorporated into the probe  uh i just want to make sure it  understood the question is regarding to  the oxygen sensing  yes the oxygen sensor okay so  um how it works so i caught the  microelectrodes with polymer  siloxane which is the soluble matrix and  these are the mysteries of  sorry matrix called the suloxin sensor  which is a siloxane we have two  different forms uh  we use raw molecular weight padmeter  suloxane  and now we use tetradeca meteoroxane and  how works that we use  a mri technique called pistol  and we detect the changes of the spin  relaxation rate  and we correlate this with the oxygen  partial pressure of the  environment  and did you answer the second part  how it how is it exactly incorporated  into the pose  oh okay so what we do uh we  emerge the microelectrodes in the  siloxane sensor  over time so then the matrix uptakes the  sensor  okay thank you  great thanks so much uh this question is  for edward i believe  um regarding the probes can you say  anything about the dimensions of the  probes and you know what you think the  actual limits will be  uh yes that's an excellent question the  probe